A module can have one default export imported with any name, plus many named exports imported with their exact names in braces.

code:
export default class User {}
export const VERSION = '1.0';
// import User, { VERSION } from './user.js';

real example:
Test configs frequently default-export the config object and name-export helpers and constants.

package.json is the manifest for a Node project, defining name, version, scripts, and dependencies so npm and frameworks know how to run the project.

code:
{
  "name": "web-tests",
  "scripts": { "test": "playwright test" },
  "dependencies": { "@playwright/test": "^1.40.0" }
}

real example:
CI pipelines run npm ci then npm test, which reads scripts and installed versions straight from package.json.

Dynamic import loads a module on demand with import(expression), returning a promise, useful for heavy features that are not needed at startup.

code:
const { chart } = await import('./heavy-chart.js');
chart.render();

real example:
Lazy-loaded modules only exist on the page after the import resolves, which changes what an automation scan sees at load time.

Side-effect imports run their top-level code on load, so ordering determines when global patches, styles, or polyfills apply.

code:
import './styles.css';
import './polyfills.js';

real example:
Injecting a mock module before the app entry point in test setups mirrors this ordering behavior to override globals early.

ESM is the modern standard with static imports and async loading, while CommonJS is Node's older synchronous system with require and module.exports.

code:
// ESM
import x from './x.js';
export default x;
// CommonJS
const x = require('./x');
module.exports = x;

real example:
Some testing tools are CommonJS-only, so team configs sometimes choose .cjs extensions to keep one consistent system.
